# RP-2.1: Surgical AST Patching and JSON Diagnostics

## Abstract
This paper presents a theoretical framework for surgical Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) patching and JSON diagnostics in the openOODA system. The Observe, Orient, Decide, Act (OODA) loop fails when artificial intelligence agents must process human-oriented error text. This research introduces a dual-surface diagnostic architecture that provides machine-readable, AST-oriented fixes. This system enables coding agents to perform deterministic, single-step code repairs without full-file read and write operations.

## 1. Introduction

The OODA loop is a conceptual model for continuous feedback. It contains four steps: Observe, Orient, Decide, and Act. Artificial intelligence agents use this loop to repair software. However, these agents fail when they read human-oriented error text. Agents cannot easily find file locations or change large text buffers. Machine-readable diagnostics give stable codes and optional fix instructions. These diagnostics close the Observe and Orient gap.

Without this feature, the system experiences multi-turn thrashing. Agents only see unstructured text. They must read full files again. They create incorrect patches and use many context tokens. Each compilation failure increases the OODA cycle time. Unstable routing also occurs. Free-text messages change frequently. Agents cannot correctly branch their logic based on text changes. Furthermore, unsafe automatic apply mechanisms cause damage. Diagnostics need structured locations and safe patch tools. Without them, an automatic fix changes the full file. This causes high risk for capability-sensitive code. Finally, there is a human and agent mismatch. Humans want narrative diagnostics. Agents want codes, spans, and suggested edits. One product must give both without software bloat.

The success criteria for this theoretical design mandate that an agent receives only structured JSON output and reflection metadata. For common failures, the agent must make a local edit. The verification tool must accept this edit. The agent must not send the full source code to the model two times.

## 2. Related Work

Classical automated program repair uses different techniques. Search-based repair uses genetic search across program versions with test suites. Patches are usually AST-level mutations. Correctness is weak because passing tests do not mean a semantic fix. This shows that AST-scoped edits are easier to process than free-form text. Semantics-based repair uses symbolic execution and constraint solving. This creates expressions at fault locations. This gives stronger guarantees when specifications exist. But, it uses many resources and is specific to a language. Template and learning-based repair uses learned edit patterns. It predicts the type of patch. It still needs localization and validation.

Recent surveys group Large Language Model (LLM) repair into fine-tuning, prompting, procedural pipelines, and agentic frameworks. Retrieval and analysis augmented generation uses error traces, code coverage, and static facts. These improve patch quality better than raw source code. Compiler-in-the-loop systems generate, compile, and repair iteratively. Autonomous agents use the LLM as an agent with tools. They do not use it as a one-shot patch generator. Model-as-judge filters decrease overfit patches.

Commercial systems use a common loop of diagnostics, structured edit, and revalidation. They are different in their edit format and editor lock-in. Compiler diagnostics for tools include the Static Analysis Results Interchange Format (SARIF). This is the industry standard for static-analysis results. It gives rich data but is too heavy for a fast command-line agent loop. Language Server Protocol (LSP) diagnostics provide structured severity, range, and code data. Machine-readable modes in modern languages provide stable codes and spans. But, few output surgical AST rewrite suggestions that connect to a primary patch tool.

## 3. Architecture and Methodology

The design rationale for openOODA proposes a dual surface. This includes a human narrative and an agent JSON format. The JSON array provides stable codes, file paths, precise coordinates, and fix hints. Agents branch on the diagnostic code, not the free-text message.

The system builds fix suggestions without host model bloat. The system generates structured suggestions consumed by the patch mechanism. It produces a true AST edit list that includes node identifiers and replacement operations. Hints are deterministic tables. This keeps the compiler pure and self-hosted.

The interaction with the agent stack follows a strict sequence. The reflection tool provides cheap context for the Orient phase. The check tool provides JSON errors for the Observe phase. The agent decides the edit in the Decide phase. The patch tool executes a fail-closed write in the Act phase. The check tool validates the output to close the loop. JSON diagnostics alone are incomplete without a safe Act function. Together, they form the complete agent loop.

The system enforces capability and contract awareness. Capability errors operate as primary codes. Agents learn that a missing capability is not a typographical error. Contract failures emit codes that point to requirements and guarantees. This helps the repair keep mathematical bounds instead of deleting them.

The diagnostic payload maintains strict security properties. Payloads are data, not open interfaces. The path and message are safely escaped strings. Agents must not use the message as a file path to open. Diagnostics must never execute user code.

The system prevents several failures. The stable JSON schema prevents agents from misunderstanding human errors. The explicit path and line coordinates prevent wrong-file edits. Deterministic re-checks prevent infinite regenerate loops. The system mitigates accidental capability widening through patch fail-closed mechanisms.

However, residual risks remain. The system cannot guarantee semantically correct fixes. The fix hint is guidance, not a proven patch. Overfitting to tests is a classical problem. Contracts and fuzzing decrease this risk but do not stop it. Malicious code comments or strings in the message can influence simple agents. Structured diagnostics help honest agents, but hostile agents ignore them. A parse-only or partial type check can report the wrong primary span.

## 4. Conclusion

Surgical AST patching and JSON diagnostics provide a critical interface for AI coding agents. This theoretical framework separates human narrative from machine-readable data. It enables deterministic, single-turn repairs that respect capability security and mathematical contracts. Future research must address schema versioning, multi-span diagnostics, and prompt-injection hardening. The complete AST patch engine will ultimately allow autonomous systems to operate at maximum speed within the OODA loop.

